  "content": {
    "id": "p64-q38-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "38",
    "prompt": "Differentiate $f(x)=\\dfrac{x-1}{\\sqrt{x}+1}$ by first rationalising the denominator.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$f'(x)=\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}$, for $x>0$.",
      "worked": "For $x\\geq0$ and $x\\ne1$,\n$$\\begin{aligned}f(x)&=\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x}-1)}{(\\sqrt{x}+1)(\\sqrt{x}-1)}\\\\&=\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x}-1)}{x-1}\\\\&=\\sqrt{x}-1.\\end{aligned}$$\nAt $x=1$, the original function and $\\sqrt{x}-1$ both equal $0$, so $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}-1$ for all $x\\geq0$.\n$$\\begin{aligned}f(x)&=x^{1/2}-1\\\\f'(x)&=\\frac12x^{-1/2}\\\\&=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}},\\qquad x>0.\\end{aligned}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
